# ============================================================================
# User Environment Configuration
# Save this file as user.<hostname>.<username>.env (both lowercase), e.g.
#   user.$($env:COMPUTERNAME.ToLower()).$($env:USERNAME.ToLower()).env
# File: user.hostname.username.env
# Created: 2026-10-02
# ============================================================================
# Scope: User (no admin needed)
# Applied by: scoop-boot.ps1 --clean-user-scope (also run by --apply-env and by
#             scoop-complete-install.ps1 -InstallTools, Step 7)
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# Windows resolves a variable from the User scope before the Machine scope;
# only PATH is different (Machine first, then User appended). Scoop writes
# JAVA_HOME, ANT_HOME, KOTLIN_HOME, GRADLE_USER_HOME, VCPKG_ROOT, ... and PATH
# entries into the User scope on every "scoop install" and "scoop update".
# The last installed JDK therefore wins over the system.*.env file, and
# GRADLE_USER_HOME points into C:\usr\apps\gradle instead of ~\.gradle.
#
# --clean-user-scope corrects that automatically before this file is applied:
#   1. User PATH entries inside Scoop's directories (apps, shims, persist,
#      global) are removed
#   2. User variables whose name the system.*.env sets at Machine scope are
#      removed, so the Machine value is visible again
#   3. this file is applied at User scope
#
# This file is for what you want at User scope in addition: variables that
# should NOT be visible to other accounts on the machine, or explicit removals
# of User variables that Scoop set and that the system file does not manage.
# Never put secrets (tokens, passwords) here: the file may end up in Git.
#
# Documentation: https://github.com/stotz/scoop-boot (README, "Environment files")
# ============================================================================

# ============================================================================
# EXPLICIT REMOVALS (optional; the automatic cleanup already covers names that
# the system.*.env sets). Uncomment what applies to you.
# ============================================================================
#-VCPKG_ROOT
#-BAT_CONFIG_DIR
#-CHROME_EXECUTABLE

# ============================================================================
# USER-ONLY VARIABLES
# ============================================================================
#MY_TOOL_HOME=$USERPROFILE\tools
#PATH+=$MY_TOOL_HOME\bin
